# FigDown — figures as text. Spec: https://github.com/FigDown/figdown

figdown 0.1 table
# Ethernet II frame, byte-order-on-the-wire carried by CELL ORDER (the
# `BYTE-UNIT-PACKET-BLOCKS` sanctioned pattern: a byte-unit packet is a single-row table, not
# a bitfield — no misleading bit ruler). Left-to-right cell order IS the
# order bytes appear on the wire; the strip test survives because that
# order lives in the syntax, never in geometry.
#
# Two data rows = two frame variants: untagged (Ethernet II) and 802.1Q
# tagged. The tag is INSERTED between Source MAC and EtherType — the class
# marks exactly which column is the insertion point, so a reader sees it
# without pixels.
title "Ethernet II Frame — Untagged vs 802.1Q Tagged"

# Semantic class: the 802.1Q tag and its insertion point. The label states
# the MEANING; the legend derives from this line (spec §2.7).
class tag "802.1Q VLAN tag (4 bytes), inserted after Source MAC; absent in an untagged frame" fill=#dbeafe

table eth "Ethernet II frame — column order = wire order (byte counts in the second header tier)"
| Variant  | Destination MAC | Source MAC | 802.1Q Tag  | EtherType | Payload         | FCS       |
| (frame)  | (6 bytes)       | (6 bytes)  | (4 bytes)   | (2 bytes) | (46-1500 bytes) | (4 bytes) |
|----------|-----------------|------------|-------------|-----------|-----------------|-----------|
| Untagged | DA              | SA         | (none)      | Type      | Data            | CRC-32    |
| Tagged   | DA              | SA         | TPID 0x8100 + PCP/DEI/VID | Type | Data       | CRC-32    |
width 90,auto,auto,auto,auto,auto,auto
cell (1,4) class=tag
cell (2,4) class=tag

# HONEST LIMIT (`CONTIGUOUS-RANGE-GROUPING`, span/coverage annotation): the FCS (CRC-32) is
# computed over DA through Payload inclusive — a coverage span across a
# CONTIGUOUS run of columns. FigDown has no construct to draw or annotate
# such a span (curly-brace grouping over a contiguous field/cell range is
# `CONTIGUOUS-RANGE-GROUPING`, v0.2; a `cell` note is not available in the table genre). Until it
# lands, this fact rides on a source comment and the guide/showcase.md prose, never on
# geometry — stated here so a reader recovers it from the text, not pixels.
